        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        A ceramide moisturizer helps rebuild the fatty layer that keeps water in the skin, which is why it often does more for tight, stinging or flaky skin than adding another active.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            If your skin feels tight, stings when you apply products or flakes around the nose and cheeks, a ceramide moisturizer is a better first step than a stronger active. Ceramides are lipids that support the skin barrier, and creams that contain them have improved hydration and reduced water loss in studies. Once your skin feels comfortable again, add one active at a time, a few days apart, and keep the moisturizer as your constant.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When skin turns tight and reactive, the usual instinct is to reach for something stronger: an acid, a retinoid, a new brightening serum. Often the better move is the opposite. If the barrier is struggling, an active adds a job to skin that is already behind on the basics.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What your skin barrier does
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The outermost layer of skin works like a brick wall. The cells are the bricks, and a mix of fats between them is the mortar. Those fats form a seal that slows water loss and keeps irritants out, while water-holding molecules inside the cells keep the surface soft. When this system is disturbed, skin becomes dry and flaky.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14728698/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Ceramides are a major part of that mortar. They are lipids found naturally in the skin, and they matter for barrier function.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24656726/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> Skincare brands add them to moisturizers for the same reason: to help restore what the barrier is missing.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Signs your barrier needs support first
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        You do not need a test to spot a struggling barrier. These are the usual signs:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Tightness after washing.</strong> Skin feels stretched within a few minutes of cleansing and eases only when you moisturise.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Stinging.</strong> Products that were fine last month, such as a plain lotion or sunscreen, now tingle or burn.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Flaking and rough patches.</strong> This often shows up around the nose, mouth and cheeks, and makeup or sunscreen sits unevenly on top.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Shine with tightness.</strong> Oily skin can still be short on water, so an oily T-zone and tight cheeks can happen together.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If any of these sound familiar, the next few weeks are about comfort first. Dermatologists also advise seeing a dermatologist if dryness becomes painful, cracked, bleeding or very irritating, or if a good moisturizer does not help.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-basics/dry/pick-moisturizer" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
            The LNGVTY system
        </p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;color:#FFFFFF;font-size:31px;line-height:1.15;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.8px;">
            Choose by skin concern
        </h2>
        <p style="margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;">
            Skin that feels tight, dull or dehydrated points to the serum below, which pairs hydration with five ceramides and cholesterol.
        </p>

