    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What a ceramide moisturizer actually does
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The research on ceramide creams is encouraging for hydration and barrier strength, though the studies are small and tested specific products.
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hydration that lasts.</strong> In a test of a single application on healthy skin, a ceramide cream raised hydration, and at 24 hours hydration was higher than with three reference moisturizers. It also reduced water loss through the skin.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30410378/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>A stronger barrier.</strong> In 34 adults with dry, eczema-prone skin, four weeks of a cream containing ceramides, triglycerides and cholesterol improved barrier measurements and lowered skin sensitivity to an irritant, compared with a reference emollient.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34921679/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Note that the second study involved eczema-prone skin and a specific formula, so it does not promise the same result for every moisturizer or every skin type. It does show why a ceramide-containing cream is a sensible first step when the barrier is the problem.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How to choose one
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Ceramides on the ingredient list.</strong> Dermatologists suggest looking for ceramides, which help hydrate skin, and hyaluronic acid, which also holds water.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-basics/dry/pick-moisturizer" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Fragrance-free.</strong> Fragrance, alcohol and alpha hydroxy acids can irritate skin that is already struggling.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-basics/dry/pick-moisturizer" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>A non-comedogenic label if you are oily or acne-prone.</strong> This means the formula is not meant to block pores.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-basics/dry/pick-moisturizer" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>A texture you will actually use.</strong> A lighter gel-cream suits oily or humid days, and a richer cream suits dry or air-conditioned rooms. The best moisturizer is the one you are comfortable using at least twice a day.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-basics/dry/pick-moisturizer" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A barrier-first routine
    </h2>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse gently.</strong> Use a mild, fragrance-free cleanser with lukewarm water, once or twice a day.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Add a hydrating serum if you like.</strong> Apply it on damp skin before your moisturizer. A serum adds hydration, but it does not replace a moisturizer.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Moisturise morning and night.</strong> A ceramide moisturizer is the step doing the barrier work.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Finish with sunscreen in the morning.</strong> A damaged barrier is more reactive, so choose a sunscreen that does not sting.</li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If you want a serum in this routine, <a href="/products/deep-hydration-glow-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Deep Hydration, Glow Serum</a> is built for damp skin and contains five ceramides with cholesterol. The routine runs cleanse, 3 to 4 drops on damp skin, moisturise, and sunscreen in the morning. Patch test first, and check with a doctor if you are pregnant or breastfeeding.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            One product change at a time makes it possible to tell what helps. If you add a new active and your skin stings, stop it for a week, return to cleanser, moisturizer and sunscreen, and try again more slowly.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        When to bring actives back
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Once skin feels comfortable by afternoon and stops stinging, you can start adding actives again. The slow way is the safe way:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">Add one active at a time, three or four days apart.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">Start two or three nights a week and build up only if skin stays calm.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">Keep the moisturizer and sunscreen exactly as they are while you test.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Moisturizer or active first?
    </h2>

